{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788185591,"nonce":"Uf+9BRgZew7+talLmFm3UvaqLCk=","title":"pawns returns colonists in an unstable order, so any acceptance keyed on roster index is flaky","message":"Found during 3.4's verification on the bench, 2026-08-31.\n\n`pawns {filter:\"colonist\"}` returned a DIFFERENT order for the same colony\nacross two calls in one session. Spawning a pawn was enough to reorder the\nexisting entries — the new pawn did not simply append.\n\nWhy it matters beyond 3.4\n\n3.4's acceptance phase 0 takes `roster[0]` as the actor **A** and `roster[1]` as\nthe patient **B**, and every later phase is written against those. When the\norder moved, the actor became a colonist incapable of hauling and six checks\nfailed with no direct explanation. I worked around it by making every colonist\nin the fixture suitable, which is a workaround, not a fix: it means the fixture\nhas to be over-constrained forever, and it does not survive anyone writing a\nfixture that has one specialist in it.\n\n**3.6 and 4.3 will hit this the same way.** Any acceptance that says \"the first\ncolonist\" is riding an index the observer does not promise. So is any agent\nstrategy that remembers \"pawn 0 is my hauler\" across calls.\n\nThe decision to make, and it is one or the other\n\n1. **`pawns` promises an order** — by `thingIDNumber`, or by spawn order, or by\n   name — and documents it. Cheap, and it makes every existing consumer correct\n   without touching them. `thingIDNumber` is stable for a pawn's lifetime and is\n   already the id the protocol uses everywhere else, so it is the obvious key.\n2. **Consumers stop keying on position** and select by predicate instead — 3.4's\n   phase 0 would pick \"the first colonist with Hauling and Doctor enabled\". More\n   honest about intent, but it has to be done in every consumer, and it does not\n   help an agent that wants a stable handle between calls.\n\nThese are not exclusive; (1) is worth doing regardless because an observer that\nreturns an unstable order is hard to build anything on, and (2) is the right\nshape for acceptance scripts specifically.\n\nInvestigate first: find what `pawns` actually enumerates (`mapPawns.FreeColonists`\nor similar) and whether RimWorld promises anything about that list's order. The\nanswer decides whether (1) is a sort we add or an order we can simply document.\n\nAcceptance\n\n- `pawns` returns colonists in a documented, reproducible order, and the\n  documentation says what the key is.\n- A test demonstrates it: read the roster, spawn a pawn, read again, and show\n  the pre-existing entries in the same relative order.\n- The decision and its reasoning land in DESIGN.md's decisions log, and any\n  acceptance script that keys on a roster index either keeps doing so knowingly\n  or is changed to select by predicate.","files":null}]}